// Regression test for empty-string lookup-table recovery.
//
// __hexnan (newlib) classifies hex chars with `__hexdig[c]`, a 256-byte byte
// table at 0x8440 whose entry [0] is 0x00.  Ghidra references the table by its
// base address with a char-pointer type.  Two bugs used to corrupt this:
//
//   1. PcodeSerializer read a null-terminated "string" at the base; because
//      __hexdig[0] == 0x00 the value came back EMPTY, so the operand serialized
//      as {kind:"string", string_value:""} -- destroying the table.  The fix
//      emits the underlying global instead (see this fixture: the base is
//      serialized as {kind:"global", global:"ram:00008440"}).
//
//   2. The emitter then reinterpret-narrowed the array global to the 4-byte
//      access width (`*(unsigned int*)&table`) instead of decaying it to its
//      declared pointer type, so `table[index]` degraded to byte soup.  The fix
//      decays an array global to its pointer type when the varnode's own type is
//      a pointer.
//
// Together they must yield a clean `__hexdig[index]` lookup with no
// narrow_aggregate refusal and no Clang diagnostics.
//
// RUN: bash %strip-json-comments %s > %t.json
// RUN: %patchir-decomp -input %t.json -print-tu -output %t >> /dev/null 2>&1
// RUN: test -e %t.c
// RUN: %file-check -vv -check-prefix=CCHECK %s --input-file %t.c
//
// The recovered table is a named global byte array (serializer fix #1):
// CCHECK: extern unsigned char s_____00008440{{\[}}256{{\]}};
//
// ... indexed as a normal subscript (emitter fix #2), never reinterpreted:
// CCHECK: = s_____00008440{{\[}}
// CCHECK-NOT: {{\(}}unsigned int {{\*}}{{\)}}&s_____00008440
// CCHECK-NOT: {{\(}}char {{\*}}{{\)}}{{"}}{{"}}
